{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016r4uTZges1WxZKogNgi5vT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to generate detailed predicted results for each planned experiment in the experiment plan, under the \"median\" tier assumptions. I need to provide specific metric predictions with rationales for each experiment.\n\nLet me go through each experiment and predict what would happen under the median tier:\n\n**Median tier key characteristics:**\n- Δlz* detectable but medium effect size (Cohen's d 0.8–2.0)\n- Works at ≥15% contamination + ≥5K items\n- 5% contamination AUROC < 0.70\n- SFT generalization partially smooths signal\n- Leaderboard per-item data partially available (100–500 models)\n\n\n- IRT-FIT AUROC runs 10-20% below ConStat, with cross-model consistency only partial\n\nNow I'll work through the experiments systematically. I've already covered exp-two-sided, so I'm moving on to exp-extended-sim with its higher contamination levels, then the controlled contamination pilot and full experiment with Qwen3.5-9B across MMLU, followed by the analytical power derivation, leaderboard data availability research, and the large-scale leaderboard audit.\n\nI also need to tackle the 4PL versus 3PL comparison, baseline comparisons against ConStat and Min-K%++, multiscale lzm implementation, post-RL hypothesis testing, and additional benchmarks like BBH and MATH, plus domain-level analysis across MMLU.\n\nFiltering out the completed experiments, I'm focusing on the planned ones and thinking through what each requires, starting with exp-extended-sim and its inverted-U pattern at the higher contamination thresholds.\n\nFor exp-extended-sim, I'm looking at how lz* transitions from strongly negative to positive as contamination increases, with the shift becoming more pronounced at high levels. The two-sided power analysis shows negative detection peaks in the mid-range while positive detection kicks in at the highest contamination levels, and with full contamination the effect moderates. The inverted-U fit should land in that 0.75-0.90 R² range.\n\nFor exp-ctrl-contam-pilot, I'm running Qwen3.5-9B across clean, 15% contaminated, and 50% contaminated MMLU data. The effect size should be medium under the median tier, with Cohen's d around 0.8-1.5 when moving from clean to 15% contamination on the large MMLU dataset.\n\nMoving to exp-ctrl-contam with both Qwen3.5-9B and Gemma-4-31B across six contamination levels, I'm seeing that 5% contamination is barely noticeable on MMLU, 10% is borderline, and 15%+ becomes clearly detectable, though smaller benchmarks show weaker signals and SFT generalization partially masks the effect.\n\nFor exp-power-theory, I'm deriving the theoretical relationship between statistical power and sample size, and while the theory fits simulations well with that target R² range, real data shows higher variance than predicted.\n\nFor exp-leaderboard-data-recon, I'm working with 100-500 models that have per-item performance data available.\n\nThe leaderboard audit flags 10-40% of models relative to background rates, the 4PL ablation shows the d parameter has modest impact compared to 3PL, and IRT-FIT underperforms ConStat and Min-K%++ by 10-20% AUROC.\n\nCross-benchmark aggregation in exp-multiscale-lzm helps but doesn't dramatically improve detection, post-RL experiments show the contamination signal partially survives but gets attenuated, and BBH and MATH fill the gap between small and large benchmarks.\n\nNow looking at domain-level patterns in MMLU where I'm seeing some concentration effects emerge.\n\nWriting YAML format...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11788,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11788},"output_tokens":26,"service_tier":"standard","inference_